These are comprehensive study notes “Deep Dive into LLMs like ChatGPT”. The video explains how an LLM is built—from internet text, to tokens, to neural-network training and post-training—and how to use these systems while accounting for their strengths and limitations.

1. Core idea
An LLM is a neural network trained to repeatedly answer one task:

Given the tokens seen so far, what is the most likely next token?

ChatGPT-like systems may appear to reason, remember facts, converse, code, or write creatively. But at the mechanical level, they generate one token at a time from a probability distribution conditioned on the preceding context.

A useful high-level pipeline is:

Internet text
→
cleaned dataset
→
tokens
→
pretrained base model
→
post-trained assistant
→
chat/inference
Internet text→cleaned dataset→tokens→pretrained base model→post-trained assistant→chat/inference
The major stages are:

Pretraining data: collect and clean vast quantities of text.

Tokenization: transform text into discrete IDs the model can process.

Pretraining: train a Transformer to predict the next token.

Post-training: make the base model helpful, safe, and conversational.

Inference: generate an answer token by token at use time.

2. Pretraining data
What models learn from
The initial training corpus is largely text gathered from publicly available internet sources, alongside selected high-quality sources such as books, reference material, code, and other datasets. The objective is not simply to collect the largest amount of text; the dataset must also be diverse and high quality.

We use Hugging Face’s FineWeb as a representative public example. FineWeb is about 44 TB of processed text and approximately 15 trillion tokens. It illustrates that training data preparation is a substantial engineering and quality-control effort, not merely “download the internet.”

Common Crawl
A key raw source is Common Crawl, an organization that continually crawls and indexes web pages. Raw web data is noisy: it includes HTML markup, navigation bars, spam, duplicated pages, malware-related content, advertisements, boilerplate, and material of varying quality.

Therefore, raw crawl data needs extensive processing before it becomes useful model-training material.

Data-cleaning pipeline
Typical data processing includes:

Step	Purpose
URL/domain filtering	Exclude known spam, malware, adult, low-quality, or otherwise undesirable sites
Text extraction	Strip HTML, scripts, menus, repeated headers, sidebars, and formatting to retain useful document text
Language filtering	Keep or prioritize languages according to the model’s intended capabilities
Quality filtering	Remove low-information, automatically generated, repetitive, or poor-quality documents
Deduplication	Avoid training repeatedly on identical or near-identical content
Personally identifiable information filtering	Try to detect and remove sensitive personal information such as addresses or identification-like data
Important implication: data shapes capability
A model’s language skills, subject knowledge, biases, style, and blind spots are partly determined by what was retained in its training corpus.

For example:

A corpus heavily weighted toward English will tend to yield better English performance than performance in underrepresented languages.

A corpus with large amounts of code will usually improve coding ability.

Filtering decisions affect both quality and what kinds of knowledge the model has access to.

A model cannot reliably know material absent from, weakly represented in, or added after its effective training cutoff.

3. Tokenization
Text is not fed in as words
Neural networks need discrete symbols represented numerically. LLMs do not fundamentally read sentences as humans do, and they do not necessarily split text into whole words.

Instead, text is transformed into tokens—small pieces of text, each assigned an integer ID.

A token may represent:

A whole common word, such as hello

A word fragment, such as ing or tion

A space-plus-word pattern, such as world

Punctuation, a number, a newline, or a code fragment

Occasionally a single character or an unusual byte sequence

Why use tokens?
At the lowest level, a computer can represent text as bytes, but byte-by-byte input creates very long sequences. LLMs therefore use a larger vocabulary of recurring text pieces, allowing them to process text in fewer steps.

This creates a trade-off:

Small vocabulary: fewer possible symbols but much longer sequences.

Large vocabulary: more possible symbols but shorter sequences.

Modern LLM tokenizers commonly have vocabularies on the order of tens of thousands to around 100,000 tokens. The GPT-4 tokenizer example discussed in the video has 100,277 tokens.

Byte Pair Encoding
A common tokenization method is Byte Pair Encoding (BPE).

Simplified process:

Begin from byte-level units.

Identify adjacent token pairs that occur frequently.

Merge a frequent pair into a new token.

Repeat many times.

Build a vocabulary containing commonly recurring text patterns.

The result is a compression-like representation optimized for common text patterns.

Why tokenization matters
Tokenization affects:

Cost, because many APIs bill by input and output tokens.

Context-window usage.

Performance across languages and writing systems.

Performance on spelling, arithmetic formatting, rare names, and strange character sequences.

The model’s tendency to struggle with some character-level tasks.

A useful rule: the model sees token IDs, not letters or words in the way a person does.

That is why an LLM may write eloquent essays but sometimes make unexpectedly basic mistakes when asked to reverse a word, count letters, or manipulate an unusual spelling.

4. The pretrained model
The next-token prediction objective
After tokenization, training repeatedly presents the model with a sequence of tokens—the context—and asks it to predict which token comes next.

Example:

text
Context: “The capital of France is”
Target next token: “ Paris”
The model outputs a probability for every possible vocabulary token:

P
(
next token
∣
previous tokens
)
P(next token∣previous tokens)
If the vocabulary has roughly 100,000 tokens, the model produces a distribution across approximately 100,000 next-token candidates.

During training:

The actual next token is known because it appears in the source text.

The model is scored on how much probability it assigned to the true next token.

Its parameters are adjusted to increase the probability of the actual continuation and decrease probability assigned to less appropriate alternatives.

This happens across enormous numbers of examples.

Parameters: the model’s learned settings
A neural network contains parameters (often called weights). They begin as essentially random values.

Training changes those parameters gradually so that the model’s predictions better match the statistical patterns in the training data.

A helpful analogy is that parameters are enormous numbers of adjustable knobs. Training finds a configuration that captures regularities in language, code, facts, styles, reasoning patterns, dialogue, and many other structures present in the data.

The knowledge is not typically stored as a neat database of sentences. Instead, it is distributed across the model’s learned numerical parameters.

What “learning” means here
The model is not explicitly given a universal rulebook for grammar, physics, programming, or history. It learns statistical structure from vast numbers of examples.

This process can lead it to develop useful internal representations of:

Grammar and syntax

Facts frequently represented in training data

Common reasoning patterns

Programming languages and APIs

Styles of writing

Relationships among concepts

Patterns in dialogue and instruction following

But the same setup also explains why a model can produce convincing falsehoods: it is optimized to generate plausible next tokens, not inherently to verify every statement against external reality.

5. Transformer architecture
Most modern LLMs use the Transformer architecture.

At a high level, the model takes token IDs, turns them into numerical vectors, processes them through many layers, and finally produces scores for all possible next tokens.

Major components
Component	Role
Token embeddings	Convert token IDs into dense numeric vectors
Positional information	Enables the model to account for token order
Attention	Lets tokens selectively use information from other earlier tokens
MLP/feed-forward layers	Transform and combine learned features
Residual connections and normalization	Help information and gradients flow through deep networks
Output layer + softmax	Converts final scores into next-token probabilities
Attention
Attention is central to why Transformers work well with language.

When processing a token, attention lets the model assign varying importance to relevant earlier tokens. For example, when generating a pronoun, verb, variable name, or closing bracket, it can use earlier context that helps determine a suitable continuation.

Attention does not make the model literally read, understand, or consciously focus in the human sense. It is a learned mathematical mechanism for combining information across positions in the sequence.

Context window
The context window is the maximum number of tokens the model can consider in one inference pass.

This matters because the model has no unlimited memory of an ongoing conversation. It operates on the tokens currently included in its context.

A longer context window can support:

Longer documents

More conversation history

Larger codebases

More examples and instructions

Retrieval of details placed earlier in the prompt

But context is still finite, can be expensive, and may be imperfectly used—especially when crucial information is buried in a very large prompt.

6. Training vs. inference
Training
During training:

The model is exposed to massive token sequences.

Its parameters are updated repeatedly.

This process requires substantial computing infrastructure and time.

The aim is to encode useful predictive structure into the weights.

Inference
During inference—when you chat with a completed model:

The weights are normally fixed.

Your prompt becomes part of the context.

The model predicts a distribution over possible next tokens.

A token is selected.

That new token is appended to the context.

The process repeats until the response stops.

So a chat response is an autoregressive generation loop:

Prompt
→
next token
→
next token
→
⋯
Prompt→next token→next token→⋯
The model does not typically retrain itself from your individual message while responding.

7. Sampling and generation
The model does not always choose the single most probable token. Instead, a sampling method may choose among likely candidates.

This is why identical prompts can produce different answers.

Sampling trade-off
Setting tendency	Typical effect
More deterministic / lower randomness	More consistent, conservative, repetitive answers
More random / higher randomness	More diverse, creative, surprising, but sometimes less reliable answers
A deterministic mode can be useful for structured extraction, code transformation, and precise formatting. More variation may help brainstorming, creative writing, or generating several alternative approaches.

The output is not usually a direct copy of one training document. Because choices are made token by token, generation is commonly a novel recombination of patterns learned during training.

8. GPT-2 as a historical example
Karpathy uses GPT-2 as an approachable example of a recognizably modern LLM stack.

The key lesson is not its exact size, but the scaling trend:

GPT-2 used a Transformer architecture.

It had about 1.6 billion parameters in the example discussed.

Its context length was 1,024 tokens.

It was trained on roughly 100 billion tokens.

Modern frontier systems generally use much larger datasets, substantially larger models, longer context windows, improved data curation, and more capable training and inference infrastructure.

The broader point: many ideas behind today’s models existed in earlier systems, but capability improved as models, data, hardware, software, and post-training techniques scaled.

9. Base models vs. assistants
Base model
A base model is primarily trained to continue text. Given a prompt, it continues in the style and patterns suggested by that prompt.

It can be capable, but it may:

Continue a document rather than answer a question.

Imitate undesirable content.

Follow patterns from the prompt in unexpected ways.

Lack a stable assistant-like interaction style.

Post-trained assistant
A chat assistant is usually a base model that receives additional training intended to make it more useful in conversation.

Post-training teaches behavior such as:

Answer the user’s question directly.

Follow instructions.

Write clear explanations.

Use a cooperative assistant tone.

Decline certain unsafe requests.

Use tools where applicable.

Structure answers appropriately.

This distinction is crucial: pretraining creates broad capability; post-training shapes behavior.

10. Post-training data
Post-training relies more on curated conversations and demonstrations than the massive raw internet corpus used for pretraining.

Examples include:

A user asks a question and an expert writes an ideal answer.

A user requests code and a high-quality code response is supplied.

A harmful or impossible request is paired with an appropriate refusal or safe alternative.

Multiple possible answers are ranked by human evaluators.

The model learns not only information patterns but also a desired interaction policy: what an assistant should say, how it should say it, and when it should ask for clarification, refuse, or use a tool.

11. Supervised fine-tuning
Supervised fine-tuning (SFT) trains the model on example prompt-response pairs.

Example:

text
User: Explain photosynthesis to a 12-year-old.
Assistant: Photosynthesis is the process plants use to turn sunlight,
water, and carbon dioxide into food...
The model learns to imitate high-quality answers.

Strengths of SFT
Gives the model an assistant-like format.

Improves instruction following.

Makes outputs more coherent and useful.

Establishes baseline safety and communication behavior.

Is relatively straightforward: train the model to reproduce desired responses.

Limitation of SFT
SFT alone can be limiting because it primarily teaches the model to imitate demonstrations. It does not necessarily optimize behavior across complex, multi-step goals or teach the model to explore better strategies beyond the examples provided.

That motivates reinforcement-learning-based post-training.

12. Hallucinations and reliability
What is a hallucination?
A hallucination is an output that sounds confident and plausible but is false, fabricated, unsupported, or incorrectly inferred.

This is not merely an occasional interface bug. It follows from the core setup: the model is generating likely text based on patterns, rather than consulting a guaranteed truth database.

Why hallucinations happen
Common causes include:

The model lacks the requested fact in its learned parameters.

The answer requires current or private information unavailable in the prompt.

The prompt is ambiguous or underspecified.

The model is pressured to answer rather than permitted to say “I don’t know.”

It combines familiar fragments into an incorrect but fluent statement.

It makes an error in a long chain of reasoning.

The desired output format encourages a fabricated citation, source, or detail.

Practical response
Treat LLM output as a capable draft or reasoning aid—not as automatic evidence.

For high-stakes tasks, verify:

Exact claims

Statistics

Legal, medical, financial, and safety guidance

Code behavior and security

Citations and quotations

Current events and product details

Names, dates, source links, and references

13. Tools, knowledge, and memory
A base LLM has knowledge distributed in its parameters, but it is not inherently connected to live external sources.

Tools can extend it.

Capability	What it provides
Web search/browsing	Access to current public information and sources
Retrieval over documents	Grounding in provided files, manuals, or organizational knowledge
Code execution	Reliable calculations, data processing, testing, and structured analysis
APIs	Access to databases, calendars, email, inventory, maps, or other systems
Long-term memory systems	Storage and retrieval of selected user preferences or prior facts
Key distinction: context vs. memory
Context is the text currently supplied in the model’s input window.

Working memory is effectively the information the model can use from that current context.

Long-term memory requires an external system that stores and retrieves information across interactions.

Parametric knowledge is the knowledge compressed into model weights during training.

The model’s context is not equivalent to a human’s persistent memory. If information is omitted, truncated, buried, or unavailable, the model may fail to use it.

14. Models need tokens to think
A central point in the video is that models often need generated tokens to perform extended reasoning.

A model that outputs an answer immediately may have little room for a multi-step calculation, proof, planning process, or search through alternative approaches. Allowing intermediate reasoning tokens can improve performance on harder tasks.

Conceptually:

More useful intermediate computation
→
more tokens/time
→
potentially stronger final answer
More useful intermediate computation→more tokens/time→potentially stronger final answer
This is why some models are designed or trained to spend more inference-time computation on difficult questions.

However, longer reasoning is not automatically correct. It can still contain flawed assumptions, and users should evaluate the final result and verify important claims.

15. Tokenization revisited: spelling limits
LLMs can struggle with spelling and other character-level operations because they process tokens rather than individual letters.

For a model, a familiar word might be represented as one token or a few chunks. That means the internal representation does not naturally correspond to a sequence of separate characters.

This explains seemingly surprising failures such as:

Counting a specific letter in a word.

Reversing a string exactly.

Manipulating rare names.

Preserving complicated formatting.

Handling arbitrary symbol sequences.

Consistently following character-by-character constraints.

Better prompting for exact text tasks
When character-level accuracy matters:

Ask the model to work one character at a time.

Specify exact formatting constraints.

Request a table or numbered intermediate steps.

Use a code tool for counting, matching, parsing, validation, or transformation.

Independently verify the final output.

16. Jagged intelligence
LLM intelligence is jagged, not uniformly human-like.

A model can perform impressively on a difficult intellectual task and then fail on something a person would find trivial. Its performance is uneven across tasks, phrasing, languages, formats, and levels of novelty.

Examples of this unevenness:

It may draft sophisticated code but miss an obvious edge case.

It may explain a scientific concept well but invent a specific citation.

It may solve a complex-looking puzzle yet fail at exact letter counting.

It may be excellent at a common language but weaker in a low-resource language.

It may produce a compelling plan but overlook a practical constraint not included in the prompt.

The right mindset is not “the system is smart” or “the system is dumb.” Instead:

It is a powerful but uneven tool whose ability depends strongly on the task, data, context, prompting, tools, and verification method.

17. Reinforcement learning
Why reinforcement learning?
In supervised learning, the model imitates example answers. In reinforcement learning (RL), the model is encouraged to generate responses that receive high reward under some evaluation system.

The general loop is:

The model generates an answer.

A reward signal evaluates it.

Training adjusts the model to favor better-performing behaviors.

The process repeats across many examples.

The reward may be generated by:

Human judgments

A learned reward model trained from human preferences

Automated verifiers, such as a math checker, unit tests, a game score, or compiler feedback

A combination of the above

RL can help models learn behavior
RL is useful where outcomes can be evaluated even if there is no single obvious “demonstration” to imitate.

Examples:

Improving helpfulness and instruction following.

Choosing more preferred answers.

Solving verifiable mathematics or programming tasks.

Improving tool use.

Learning to plan or reason over multiple steps.

Playing games with clearly measurable success.

18. DeepSeek-R1 and reasoning models
Karpathy discusses DeepSeek-R1 as an illustration of a recent class of models that place greater emphasis on long-form reasoning behavior and reinforcement learning.

The broader takeaway is that strong performance on difficult reasoning tasks may come not only from a larger pretrained model, but also from:

Incentives to explore solutions.

Rewards for verifiable correctness.

Generating longer chains of intermediate reasoning.

Using more inference-time computation for difficult tasks.

Distillation or training approaches that transfer reasoning-like behavior into more efficient models.

This changes the usual expectation of a chatbot: instead of always responding instantly, a system may benefit from allocating more time and tokens to harder questions.

19. AlphaGo as an RL analogy
AlphaGo provides a useful intuition for reinforcement learning.

In a game such as Go, the system can play moves, receive a result, and use a clear success signal: winning or losing. It does not need a human to provide an ideal move for every position.

The lesson for LLMs is that RL can be especially effective when there is a reliable way to check outcomes.

For example:

In code, tests may verify whether a solution works.

In mathematics, an answer checker may confirm correctness.

In games, the score or win/loss result is measurable.

In tool-use tasks, success may be whether the correct external action was performed.

The challenge is that many real-world language tasks do not have a clean, automatic correctness signal. Human preference data and learned reward models are then often used, but they can be imperfect.

20. RLHF
Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback (RLHF) combines model generation, human preferences, and reinforcement learning.

A simplified RLHF pipeline:

Train a base model through next-token prediction.

Supervised fine-tune it on high-quality demonstrations.

Ask human evaluators to compare or rank candidate responses.

Train a reward model to predict human preferences.

Use RL to optimize the assistant toward high reward.

What RLHF aims to improve
Helpfulness

Harmlessness

Honesty or calibrated uncertainty

Instruction following

Tone and clarity

Refusal behavior

Reduced toxic, dangerous, or unwanted output

RLHF limitations
The reward system can be gamed. A model may learn to produce answers that look good to evaluators without actually being correct or deeply useful.

Potential problems include:

Sounding more confident than warranted.

Being overly verbose, flattering, or cautious.

Optimizing for apparent helpfulness rather than truth.

Refusing too broadly or not broadly enough.

Developing stylistic patterns favored by the reward process.

Therefore, post-training is a balancing problem: the goal is to create an assistant that is useful, truthful, safe, and not excessively constrained.

21. How to use LLMs well
Give sufficient context
The model can only reason from what it receives and what it learned previously. Include relevant background, constraints, examples, source material, audience, and desired output format.

Weak prompt:

text
Write a report.
Stronger prompt:

text
Write a 700-word report for a nontechnical manager explaining why
customer churn increased in Q2. Use the attached metrics, distinguish
facts from hypotheses, provide three possible causes, and end with
five recommended actions.
Define the task precisely
State:

Your desired outcome

The intended audience

Constraints and assumptions

Required format

Length

Tone

What the model should not do

Whether it should ask questions when information is missing

Use iteration
Treat the first answer as a draft. Improve it by asking for:

Corrections

Alternate approaches

Missing assumptions

Counterarguments

A concise version

A more detailed version

A table, checklist, or structured plan

An explanation of uncertainty

A critique against your requirements

Request verification behavior
For factual work, explicitly ask the model to:

Separate known facts from inferences.

Flag uncertainty.

Identify assumptions.

Cite or link sources when tools are available.

Avoid fabricating details.

Say what would need to be checked externally.

Use tools for tool-shaped tasks
Use external retrieval, calculators, code execution, databases, or documents when the task requires current, precise, or mechanically verifiable information.

Good candidates for tools:

Latest news or prices

Exact calculations

Spreadsheet analysis

Long-document extraction

Citation verification

Data cleaning

Code testing

Searching private documents

Comparing current product specifications

